1. Introduction
Several circulating autoantibodies (autoAb) reactive
against central nervous system (CNS) antigens have been
demonstrated in patients with neurological disorders. The
presence of these autoAb was correlated with specific
diseases, thus suggesting that their detection could be
introduced in clinical neurology as a diagnostic marker of
disease. This possibility, however, was weakened by recent
findings of different autoAb in the same clinical disorder
(Grimakti et al., 1993; Nemni et al., 1993).
Here we describe a patient with autoimmune diabetes
and anti-GABAergic neurons autoAb who over a different
time period developed two different neurological disorders
that were both correlated with an autoimmune pathogene-
sis.
